# ThreatPulse — access control for the API tree.
#
# Two different requirements, and only one of them is a restriction:
#
#   PUBLIC      every read endpoint, the discovery index and this documentation
#               page. The React frontend calls /api/cti/v1/* directly from the
#               visitor's browser, so these have to accept anyone or the site
#               stops working for the public.
#
#   RESTRICTED  publish.php, the write endpoint the pipeline POSTs to. Reachable
#               only from the networks listed below.
#
#   DENIED      db.php, which is a library the endpoints include server-side and
#               never something that should be served as a URL.
#
# The allowlist:
#
#   Require ip           private ranges (10/8, 192.168/16, 172.20/16)
#   Require forward-dns  the home FortiGate's dynamic DNS name
#
# forward-dns is the right provider for a dynamic address, not `Require host`:
# `host` matches on reverse DNS, a PTR lookup the DDNS provider does not
# maintain, while forward-dns resolves the name and compares the result to the
# client address. Apache documents it as the provider for clients "which use a
# dynamic DNS service". It requires httpd 2.4.19 or later.
#
# mod_authz_core is a Base module of httpd 2.4, so nothing here is wrapped in
# <IfModule>. That is deliberate: an <IfModule> guard would fail open and serve
# the write endpoint to the world, and a security control should fail closed.

Options -Indexes
DirectoryIndex index.html

# Public: the read API, the discovery index and this page. Stated explicitly
# rather than left to the server default, so that the exception below is visible
# next to what it is an exception to.
Require all granted

# Restricted: the pipeline's write endpoint.
#
# A <Files> section carries its own authorization, which applies in place of the
# directory directive above for the files it matches, so this is a genuine
# restriction and not an addition to a broader grant. <Files> matches the
# basename at any depth, so /api/cti/v1/publish.php is covered.
<Files "publish.php">
    <RequireAny>
        Require ip 10.0.0.0/8
        Require ip 192.168.0.0/16
        Require ip 172.20.0.0/16
        Require forward-dns johniliadotefiber.fortiddns.com
    </RequireAny>
</Files>

# Denied: db.php is included by the endpoints server-side. Requesting it as a
# URL returns an empty body today, and nothing should be able to try.
<Files "db.php">
    Require all denied
</Files>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    Header set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    Header set Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
    Header set X-Frame-Options "DENY"
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERIFYING IT WORKS
#
# The two endpoints answer with different statuses, which makes the rule
# self-checking: a blocked request never reaches PHP, so it cannot return 401.
#
#   from a DISALLOWED address:
#     POST /api/cti/v1/publish.php   -> 403  (Apache refused it)
#
#   from an ALLOWED address, without a token:
#     POST /api/cti/v1/publish.php   -> 401  (PHP ran and rejected the caller)
#
#   from ANYWHERE:
#     GET  /api/cti/v1/threats.php   -> 200  (still public)
#
# A 401 from a disallowed address means the restriction is NOT taking effect.
# A 403 on threats.php means the public grant above is not taking effect either.
#
# IF forward-dns IS REJECTED
# Older builds than httpd 2.4.19 have no forward-dns provider. The symptom is a
# 500 on the whole API, with "Invalid command 'Require'" or "unknown provider" in
# the error log, because a parse error takes the directory down with it. Pin the
# address instead, and re-check it whenever the DDNS name changes:
#
#     Require ip 85.75.30.118
#
# Check the current answer with:  nslookup johniliadotefiber.fortiddns.com
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

